1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,267
A page appears after one click. But the server
could be

2
00:00:05,267 --> 00:00:10,533
across an ocean, and your computer started
with only a name.

3
00:00:10,533 --> 00:00:15,800
How does that tiny action become a complete
journey? Follow this

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,067
request.

5
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:26,433
We'll slow down a first visit using HTTP
version one point

6
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:31,800
one over TCP. Browsers often reuse earlier
work. Leaving those shortcuts

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,167
aside lets us see the separate decisions
hidden inside an ordinary

8
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:42,533
page load.

9
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:47,800
Our request first crosses a local network,
then enters a chain

10
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:53,067
of other networks. The Internet is this
interconnection. No single cable

11
00:00:53,067 --> 00:00:58,333
stretches from every browser to every website;
routers join the pieces

12
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:03,600
together.

13
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:09,075
Notice how many different jobs are already
appearing. Layers separate those

14
00:01:09,075 --> 00:01:14,550
jobs. OSI describes seven; this TCP/IP picture
groups them into four.

15
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:20,025
They're different ways to organize
responsibilities, rather than physical floors
inside

16
00:01:20,025 --> 00:01:25,500
the computer.

17
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,867
The browser has a name, but packets need an
address. DNS

18
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:36,233
supplies that missing link. We ask a resolver
to find the

19
00:01:36,233 --> 00:01:41,600
address associated with the name, turning
something readable into something the

20
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,967
network can use.

21
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:52,342
Without a cached answer, the resolver follows
referrals: root, top-level domain,

22
00:01:52,342 --> 00:01:57,717
then the authoritative server. Each reply
narrows the search. No single

23
00:01:57,717 --> 00:02:03,092
directory needs to store the complete answer
for every name on

24
00:02:03,092 --> 00:02:08,467
the Internet.

25
00:02:08,467 --> 00:02:13,833
Now repeat the lookup. The saved answer avoids
the earlier search,

26
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:19,200
so the path gets shorter. A cache makes
previous work useful

27
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,567
again, but only within its allowed lifetime.
Old directions can eventually

28
00:02:24,567 --> 00:02:29,933
become wrong.

29
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:35,517
Reaching the right computer is only part of
delivery. Which application

30
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:41,100
should receive the data? Ports distinguish
endpoints there. Our client uses

31
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:46,683
49152; the server uses 80. The address and
port answer different

32
00:02:46,683 --> 00:02:52,267
questions.

33
00:02:52,267 --> 00:02:57,642
Before the request, three TCP messages cross
the link: SYN, SYN

34
00:02:57,642 --> 00:03:03,017
with ACK, then ACK. Each side establishes
connection state. The connection

35
00:03:03,017 --> 00:03:08,392
is shared agreement between endpoints; the
network hasn't reserved a private

36
00:03:08,392 --> 00:03:13,767
physical path.

37
00:03:13,767 --> 00:03:21,067
Now the browser can ask for the resource. GET
supplies the

38
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:28,367
HTTP meaning; TCP carries the bytes. Notice
the separation: transporting a

39
00:03:28,367 --> 00:03:35,667
request successfully doesn't tell us whether
the requested page exists.

40
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:41,142
Watch the blue message stay inside as new
information appears around

41
00:03:41,142 --> 00:03:46,617
it. This is encapsulation: each layer adds
what its own job

42
00:03:46,617 --> 00:03:52,092
needs. A larger message can also be divided
across several transport

43
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:57,567
segments.

44
00:03:57,567 --> 00:04:03,050
TCP adds ports and sequence information,
identifying the conversation and locating

45
00:04:03,050 --> 00:04:08,533
bytes within it. The application data remains
inside. A boundary between

46
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:14,017
application writes doesn't necessarily become
a boundary between segments on the

47
00:04:14,017 --> 00:04:19,500
wire.

48
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,975
Next come the IP source and destination.
Routers can use the

49
00:04:24,975 --> 00:04:30,450
destination to choose where the packet goes
next. The server remains

50
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:35,925
our goal here, though mechanisms such as
address translation can alter

51
00:04:35,925 --> 00:04:41,400
addresses.

52
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:47,092
The outer frame answers a more immediate
question: who receives this

53
00:04:47,092 --> 00:04:52,783
on the local link? For a distant server, that
may be

54
00:04:52,783 --> 00:04:58,475
the gateway. The next local destination and
the final IP destination

55
00:04:58,475 --> 00:05:04,167
serve different purposes.

56
00:05:04,167 --> 00:05:11,478
All those headers and bytes must now become a
physical signal.

57
00:05:11,478 --> 00:05:18,789
Copper, fiber, and radio do this differently.
The travelling marks represent

58
00:05:18,789 --> 00:05:26,100
changing signals, not tiny printed digits
being pushed through a cable.

59
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:31,683
At the switch, the frame takes a local turn
based on

60
00:05:31,683 --> 00:05:37,267
MAC forwarding information. Watch the selected
output. This decision moves data

61
00:05:37,267 --> 00:05:42,850
within the local network; it doesn't calculate
the entire route to

62
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:48,433
the website.

63
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:53,908
The router removes the incoming frame and
examines the IP destination.

64
00:05:53,908 --> 00:05:59,383
It chooses a next hop without needing to
interpret GET. A

65
00:05:59,383 --> 00:06:04,858
useful network can move many kinds of
application data using the

66
00:06:04,858 --> 00:06:10,333
same forwarding machinery.

67
00:06:10,333 --> 00:06:15,808
Here is the detail to watch: the local frame
changes, while

68
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:21,283
our request continues toward the server. Each
routed hop gets its

69
00:06:21,283 --> 00:06:26,758
own link information. One journey is being
carried through several separate

70
00:06:26,758 --> 00:06:32,233
local deliveries.

71
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:37,717
A shared outgoing link creates a bottleneck.
Packets arrive faster than

72
00:06:37,717 --> 00:06:43,200
it can send them, so a queue grows. When the
buffer

73
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:48,683
fills, packets may be dropped. Connectivity
alone doesn't promise immediate or

74
00:06:48,683 --> 00:06:54,167
successful delivery.

75
00:06:54,167 --> 00:06:59,642
At the server, the wrapping comes off in
stages: link, IP,

76
00:06:59,642 --> 00:07:05,117
TCP, then HTTP. Each layer handles its own
information and passes

77
00:07:05,117 --> 00:07:10,592
the useful contents upward. The application
finally receives the request we

78
00:07:10,592 --> 00:07:16,067
started with.

79
00:07:16,067 --> 00:07:21,867
The server returns status information and
content. Those bytes need the

80
00:07:21,867 --> 00:07:27,667
same layered delivery in reverse. But reverse
doesn't require an identical

81
00:07:27,667 --> 00:07:33,467
route: the response can cross a different
sequence of networks on

82
00:07:33,467 --> 00:07:39,267
its way back.

83
00:07:39,267 --> 00:07:44,742
That response may contain instructions to
fetch images, styles, and other

84
00:07:44,742 --> 00:07:50,217
resources. So one apparent page load becomes
several conversations, sometimes involving

85
00:07:50,217 --> 00:07:55,692
other servers or caches. The first arrival
starts another round of

86
00:07:55,692 --> 00:08:01,167
work.

87
00:08:01,167 --> 00:08:06,650
Our example used HTTP over TCP. HTTPS adds TLS
protection, while

88
00:08:06,650 --> 00:08:12,133
HTTP version three uses QUIC over UDP.
Different protocols can fill

89
00:08:12,133 --> 00:08:17,617
these roles, but the underlying questions
about names, endpoints, and delivery

90
00:08:17,617 --> 00:08:23,100
still remain.

91
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:28,575
Return to the opening click. A name became an
address, bytes

92
00:08:28,575 --> 00:08:34,050
gained headers, and routers connected local
steps into one journey. The

93
00:08:34,050 --> 00:08:39,525
remarkable part is how many small agreements
cooperate to produce something

94
00:08:39,525 --> 00:08:45,000
that feels immediate.
